notes:
These are the primary sources the csoh.org kill chain cites. Every fact about the incident in this deck comes from that page, which draws on these.

StepSecurity published the full technical teardown, and Socket the SLSA provenance analysis. Snyk is the page's source for why the fork was named zblgg/configuration, for the runner-internal token behind the cache writes, for the count above 170 and for the seven-day cooldown. Wiz counted the packages the worm reached; research published by Wiz is one of these sources, and the site's author works at Wiz. GitHub's advisory tracks the TanStack packages as CVE-2026-45321 and lists voicproducoes as an attacker account. TechCrunch reported OpenAI's downstream disclosure. The Hacker News and BleepingComputer covered the two GitHub Actions that came back in September.

The background slides name their own sources, from csoh.org, AWS and MITRE ATT&CK.

